{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices across most horizons (often by ~2–8 EUR), especially during downtrends.",
    "Downtrend underestimation (lag): the model reacts too slowly to bearish momentum; it projects stabilization/rebounds while the market continues to grind lower. Apply a trend-following downward adjustment when the last 10–20 days slope is negative.",
    "Over-optimistic rebounds: after small upticks in history, the model extrapolates a stronger bounce than realized. Damp rebound amplitude (e.g., shrink positive day-to-day changes in the forecast).",
    "Horizon effect: error grows with lead time; later-horizon forecasts drift further above outcomes. Use a horizon-increasing negative correction (larger haircut for days 15–30 than days 1–7).",
    "Volatility/peaks overshoot: local peaks in the forecast are higher and more frequent than in outcomes; cap forecast peaks relative to recent realized range (e.g., limit forecast to recent 10–15 day high plus a small buffer).",
    "Mean-reversion miscalibration: the model mean-reverts to a level above the true path. Re-anchor mean-reversion level to a lower reference (e.g., recent 20–30d average minus a bearish offset when trend is negative)."
  ]
}
